Neoklis wrote:
> I wonder whether I should refirmware to SRM

I don't have any experience with the JumpStart! cd, but I believe the
main reason to switch from AlphaBIOS to SRM is that support for
AlphaBIOS is going away.  As far as DEC was concerned it was mainly just
to boot Windows NT; SRM is what is used to boot Digital UNIX and
OpenVMS.


> I think I saw an article somewhere saying it may be possible to have both firmware
> installed.

Could you post a pointer to this article?  From my experience, you would
need two bootable drives in order to pull this off.  (More below.) 

> what dangers are there carrying out changes in firmware?

I recently switched from AlphaBIOS to SRM on my 164LX and I got bit when
I found out (too late) that my hard drive was partitioned incorrectly. 
Basically, you have to partition your boot disk in a way which is
understandable to your firmware.  This means BSD type for SRM and FAT
table for AlphaBIOS. (If you have other disks they can be partitioned
one way or another.)  Simply put: when you partition for SRM you need to
choose the 'b' request in fdisk for BSD disklabels.

Here's where it gets tricky: If you are careful, and you have at the
start of your disk a space (an old FAT partition for MILO) where you can
write your bootblock, then you can write a new disklablel and keep your
data partitions intact.  First, run fdisk and choose the 'u' request to
changes units from cylinders to sectors.  Second, print the partition
table and write down (you don't want to loose this ... trust me) the
sectors on which each partition begins and ends.  Third, choose the 'b'
request to switch to bsd mode.  Fourth, recreate your partition table
(in sectors) minus your FAT partition for MILO (this is where your
bootblock will go).  Fifth, install/configure aboot ... there are plenty
of HOWTO's for this step.

That should do it.  Although, everything I just spit out was from memory
... so re-read the HOWTO's.  If you screw up, try downloading 'gpart'
(search for it on freshmeat.net) ... it helped me recover my partitions
(and all of my data) when I found all this out too late.

BTW: In the #define for 'align' in gpart.h, you need to cast to 'long
int' instead of 'int'.

Good Luck,

Bill Welch 

p.s. I got most of my information from Michal Jaegermann ... thanks
Michal!



