Hi,

I am wondering what SCSI drives would work with this system.  The system
is apparently an AlphaServer 800 5/500 (as obtained from 'cat
/proc/cpuinfo').  It has an ISP1020 (Q Logic) SCSI controller.

The system has one hard drive -- a Seagate Baraccuda model ST34371WC
(this is what's printed on the drive; linux identifies the drive as an
ST34371W, but the drive definitely has a "WC" type interface as it is
connected to a back-plane).  Also, linux says the "ANSI SCSI Revision"
of the drive is 2 (does that mean it's a SCSI-2 drive?).

As the drive is only 4 gig, I am in the market for a new drive.  Being
new to SCSI, however, I am somewhat confused over which drives will work
with my system (and, of course, I don't want to spend a lot of money on
something I won't be able to use).  Specifically my questions are:

1) Which SCSI interfaces (e.g. SCSI-2, ULTRA SCSI, etc.) are compatible
with the ISP1020 controller?

2) Which "cable" interfaces should work with my backplane?  Obviously
the 80-pin SCA interface, but are (what seagate refers to as) the "WC",
"DC", and "LC" all compatible, or do I need to use "WC"?

Also, if anyone has any specific recommendations, those are welcome as
well (it doesn't have to plug into the back-plane, although that is
preffered).

--Nathan Davis

PS  I went to Seagate's site (www.seagate.com) to lookup the specs for
the HD, but apparently the WC model of that drive doesn't exist (only
FC, N, W, and WD).  Interesting.

PPS I went to www.qlogic.com to find information on the ISP1020.  They
at least admit it exists, but I couldn't find any documentation on it
(just documentation refering to it).



