If you did the install and did not use fdisk and bsd disk labels, you will
not be able to boot.

to install

boot "cd-device"  -fi /kernels/generic.gz -fl "root=/dev/(scd0 or hda)
expert text"
scd0 = SCSI CDrom
hda = IDE CDrom

when you get to the screen to select the type of installation you will see a
little check box in the upper right corner, label'd "fdisk"  check it off.

start your first partition at cyl 2 - to leave room for the boot image.

If you do not do this, RH uses disk druid and creates FAT style partitions,
which are not compatible with SRM

OR. use the jumpstart CD, it will create the partitions, and write the boot
image to the disk. the RH install will default to the existing BSD labels.

JJ



