>
> There are 2 ways to handle these kinds of things, the first using SRM and
> friends, the second using milo. If you're most familar with Linux on Intel,
> I'd suggest you use Milo and DOS-style partitioning. If it's supported on
> your system, switch to the ARC firmware, and follow the normal install
> instructions. Use DOS-style partitioning, but reserve a little bit of space
> at the start of the drive for a small MS-DOS partition to hold milo and
> loadlin (or was it linload?). Once you're done with the install, make that
> partition, plop milo and loadlin on it, and configure the boot menu to
> launch loadlin on milo, passing it the milo command to boot the kernel with
> the appropriate values (e.g. root=/dev/whatever, etc). This will get you a
> system that has fairly doslike partitioning, and you won't need to play with
> what kind of partition mapping you're using when you're compiling a kernel.
> Another nice thing about milo is that it lets you poke around in your filesystem
> if you want to boot from alternate kernels or similar.
>
> Hope this helps...
>
