Pat Gunn (qc@apk.net) wrote:
: Another nice thing about milo is that it lets you poke around in your filesystem
: if you want to boot from alternate kernels or similar.

You have exactly the same capabilty with aboot.  A file /etc/aboot.conf
allows you to predefine up to 10 different boot commands labelled by
single digits.  Assuming that you have set your boot device in SRM you
boot them like this 'b -fl 5' to use a line with a label "5".  If you
need more you can type directy at 'aboot>' prompt.

There are technical reasons why SRM is the preferred firmware for Linux
on Alpha (you can actually get a better performance).  Not all boards
have SRM console available or you may have a hardware (a video card, for
example) which is OK with ARC but not supported by SRM.  Then you have
to use milo - if available.

  Michal



