In comp.os.linux.alpha John Sanders <jwsander@usa.alcatel.com> wrote:
> 	What UNIX for the home market was this and what date was it available? 
> By home market, I'm assuming the 8088.  That was the first PC when UNIX
> was already in existance.  If no one did UNIX for the 8088, then when
> for the '286?
> If not the '286, then when for the '386?  And this was from who?

Well, PC-IX was available on the XT long ago.... early 80's, but did
not cut it.  I don't recall what the price was.  In 1988 IBM brought
out AIX which ran on 386 and up with 2M ram and 70mb hd and up.
It cost about $2500 for anything that included networking, C, FORTRAN,
and that kind of thing, and was quite good for its time.  There were
half a dozen others in the mid-80's, at about the same pricing.
One or two ran on 8086 and 80286, but most were geared to 80386.
Most were way too expensive for the ``home market'' unless you were
a very serious sort.  The only inexpensive thing was Coherent, and
later Minix, and then...... the floodgates opened......(:+}}.  Only
then could it be consdered ``home market''.  Tandy did have Xenix,
a V7 clone about 1981 on the old Model 16B with extra 68K hardware,
but that was not really home market material.  Heath had an LSI-11
home 16bit PC that one could get a V7ish UNIX for, but I don't
recall how expensive it was (around 2K in 1981 or so, if memory
is correct) and that was targeted at the home hobby kit market.

Aren't we all just glad that the floodgates did open?.....(:+}}...

Bob




