Hello,

My DEC AlphaStation 200 4/233, which I have already installed once before and
had working with nothing more than the usual Alpha install problems is now
giving me grief. I am running RH 6.2. A while back, I installed the 2.2.16
kernel rpm and removed all references to the .14 kernel. Well, apparenly I hosed
that process somewhere because the system was unable to boot from then on unless
I passed the name of the kernel to aboot.

Before that, I could boot the system at the SRM prompt with:

	boot dka600

and everything would go fine from there (I have never automated that process).

However, after that patch, this no longer worked. Giving the parameters, as I
said, worked, but the probelm is that was so long ago that I forgot how I did
that. 

Well, no matter, I thought, because I've been wanting to reinstall the system
anyway to change a few install options and partition a bit differently.

So, today I did just that. I reinstalled the system from the installer in text
and expert modes. Everything was installed correctly (apparently) and upon
completion, I shut the system off and turned it back on. This time, though,

	boot dka600

no longer works.

Now I get this:

	...
	jumping to bootstrap code
	aboot: Linux/Alpha SRM bootloader version 0.5
	aboot: switching to OSF/1 PALcode version 1.46
	aboot: valid disklabel found: 3 partitions.
	aboot: booted_dev=scsi 0 6 0 6 600 0 0; guessing boot_device=`scd0'
	aboot: don't know how to mount partition 1 (filesystem type 1)
	aboot: mount of partition failed
	Enter kernel name and arguments
	aboot>

What the heck? 3 partitions is correct (swap, /boot and /, in that order), but
why is it assuming that boot_device is scd0 and not sda3?

Issuing a '?' at the aboot prompt returns:

	0:2/vmlinuz-2.2.14-6.0 root=/dev/sda3

which certainly looks correct to me.

Can anyone please tell me what is wrong?

Thanks
Tad



